  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 319 PM EDT Thu Jul 30 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon upper air analysis and GOES-R water vapor \nimagery showed a flattening ridge over the central U.S. with \nshortwaves embedded in the flow. Southerly low level flow was \nbringing moisture back into the U.P. with surface analysis showing \ndewpoints in the 60s to low 70s. This combined with temperatures in \nthe 80s was making for a very warm and muggy afternoon. Instability \nwas also increasing with RAP analysis showing upwards of 2000 J/kg \nmucape. Overall shear was lacking, 25kt or less, reducing the risk \nfor organized convection. PWATs were high though, around 1.7\" over \nthe central and western U.P. A few showers and thunderstorms had \ndeveloped this afternoon with very heavy rain rates and occasional \nlightning being the main threats. Parts of the western U.P. received \nbetween 1 and 2 inches of rainfall with thunderstorms this morning \nwith up to around 3 inches near the Porcupine Mountains (radar \nestimated precip).\n\nExpect showers and thunderstorms to continue to pop up through the \nafternoon in a very weakly capped environment and also supported by \na 300mb jet streak and diffluent flow aloft. Any showers and \nthunderstorms will continue to have the threat for very heavy rain \nrates and WPC has included the western U.P. in a marginal risk for \nexcessive rainfall. As a 850mb LLJ spreads across the area there is \na chance for backbuilding of the showers and storms, which is being \npicked up by some of the CAMs. It does appear that showers and \nstorms will dissipate later this evening with the loss of daytime \nheating and upper level support. It will remain muggy tonight with \nlows in the 60s.\n\nFor Friday into the weekend a shortwave will deepen as it moves from \nthe Dakotas to the Lower Great Lakes. Much of the energy with this \nsystem will stay well south of the area. However, continued moist \nlow levels and diurnal heating will bring increasing instability \nunder a generally weak flow and low shear environment. Expect \nthunderstorms to develop in the afternoon, especially in an area of \nsurface convergence along a Lake Superior lake breeze boundary. \nWhile the risk for organized or strong convection remains very low, \nheavy rain will continue to be a threat and slow storm motions will \nincrease the risk for excessive rainfall. WPC has most of the U.P. \nin a marginal risk for excessive rain on Friday. High temperatures \nFriday will be close to seasonal averages, upper 70s to low 80s, \nthough it will remain muggy with dewpoints near 70.\n\nA cold front will push south through the area late Friday night with \n850mb temperatures falling to 8-10C by Sunday. This will bring \nnortherly winds and cooler temperatures for Saturday and Sunday with \nhighs in the mid 70s over the interior and near 70 closer to Lake \nSuperior. Most notable will be drier air for Sunday into Monday with \ndewpoints in the 50s, resulting in more comfortable conditions. A \nfew lingering showers are possible on Saturday, mainly in the south \nand east. \n\nTemperatures warm back into the 80s for Monday and Tuesday with \nhumidity increasing on Tuesday ahead of an approaching cold front. \nShowers and thunderstorms may accompany the front on Tuesday but the \nrisk for organized convection remains uncertain at this point in \ntime, with global models showing lower instability and shear.",
